pouch boundary-line patch 0045 - the main thread's stack extent from auxv (B-1b).

Supersedes the mirror 0033 put in pthread_getattr_np(): POUCH_MAIN_STACK_TOP
/ _SIZE, a hand-kept copy of the kernel's EXEC_USER_STACK_TOP / _SIZE. B-1b
raises the initial stack from 1 MiB to 8 MiB (Linux's default RLIMIT_STACK;
the browser arc's decision 4) and, rather than move the mirror, has the
kernel STATE the mapping in the auxiliary vector: AT_STACK_BASE (0x5342, the
lowest usable VA; the guard page lies below) and AT_STACK_SIZE (0x5353),
private tags above musl's AUX_CNT so they land in no libc slot (the
AT_VDSO_CLOCK precedent). One file:

  src/thread/pthread_getattr_np.c  The main-thread arm walks libc.auxv for
      the two tags and reports [base, base + size); _a_stackaddr is the TOP,
      as for a created thread (pthread_attr_getstack subtracts the size). A
      kernel that writes neither tag is refused with ENOSYS, never guessed
      (0034's rule). The two mirror constants are gone.

The pin stays two-sided on the device: pouch-hello-threads requires this
answer to equal the `stack` row of /proc/<pid>/maps (the kernel's own VMA,
read at run time) and to contain a main-thread local, and B-1b adds the
decision itself (size >= 8 MiB) and a 4 MiB frame touched at both ends,
which the old 1 MiB stack could not hold.

Kernel side (same commit): kernel/include/thylacine/elf.h (the two tags),
exec.h (EXEC_USER_STACK_SIZE 8 MiB, EXEC_INIT_AUXV_COUNT 11), exec.c
(exec_fill_auxv writes the pair from the constants it mapped with), the
exec test pins the pair against the stack VMA.

--- a/src/thread/pthread_getattr_np.c
+++ b/src/thread/pthread_getattr_np.c
@@ -1,9 +1,9 @@
 #define _GNU_SOURCE
 #include "pthread_impl.h"
+#include "libc.h"
+#include <errno.h>
+#include "_pouch_mman.h"
 
-#define POUCH_MAIN_STACK_TOP  0x0000000080000000ul
-#define POUCH_MAIN_STACK_SIZE (1024ul * 1024ul)
-
 int pthread_getattr_np(pthread_t t, pthread_attr_t *a)
 {
 	*a = (pthread_attr_t){0};
@@ -13,20 +13,31 @@
 		a->_a_stackaddr = (uintptr_t)t->stack;
 		a->_a_stacksize = t->stack_size;
 	} else {
-		/* Thylacine: the initial thread's stack is the fixed exec
-		 * mapping [TOP - SIZE, TOP), sparse demand-zero above a
-		 * one-page guard (kernel exec.h: EXEC_USER_STACK_TOP / _SIZE,
-		 * MIRRORED here; the pouch-hello-threads boot prover requires
-		 * this answer to equal the `stack` row the kernel renders in
-		 * /proc/<pid>/maps, so a stale mirror fails a boot). Upstream
-		 * finds the extent by probing downward with mremap(), which
-		 * the seam ENOSYSes -- so the loop ended on its first test and
-		 * every pouch program was told its main stack is ONE PAGE. A runtime
-		 * that sizes recursion or scans its stack from these bounds
-		 * (JavaScriptCore, a conservative GC, Rust std's main-thread
-		 * guard) then fails on its first call. */
-		a->_a_stackaddr = POUCH_MAIN_STACK_TOP;
-		a->_a_stacksize = POUCH_MAIN_STACK_SIZE;
+		/* Thylacine (B-1b, patch 0045): the initial thread's stack is the
+		 * mapping exec made, and the kernel STATES it in the auxiliary
+		 * vector -- AT_STACK_BASE (the lowest usable VA; the guard page
+		 * lies below) and AT_STACK_SIZE -- so libc DERIVES the extent
+		 * instead of mirroring the kernel's constants (0033) or probing
+		 * for it with mremap (upstream; the seam ENOSYSes it, which had
+		 * every pouch program told its main stack is one page). The
+		 * pouch-hello-threads prover requires this answer to equal the
+		 * `stack` row of /proc/<pid>/maps. A kernel that writes neither
+		 * tag is refused, never guessed: _a_stackaddr is the TOP, as for
+		 * a created thread (pthread_attr_getstack subtracts the size). */
+		uintptr_t base = 0, size = 0;
+		int have = 0;
+		for (size_t i = 0; libc.auxv[i]; i += 2) {
+			if (libc.auxv[i] == AT_THYLA_STACK_BASE) {
+				base = libc.auxv[i+1];
+				have |= 1;
+			} else if (libc.auxv[i] == AT_THYLA_STACK_SIZE) {
+				size = libc.auxv[i+1];
+				have |= 2;
+			}
+		}
+		if (have != 3 || !size) return ENOSYS;
+		a->_a_stackaddr = base + size;
+		a->_a_stacksize = size;
 	}
 	return 0;
 }
